WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.162
Injection happens when untrusted input is interpreted as instructions or structure in the wrong context.

00:00:06.162 --> 00:00:11.416
JavaScript applications face this risk in many places because they interact with databases,

00:00:11.416 --> 00:00:15.862
document stores, shells, template engines, browsers, parsers, URLs, headers, file paths,

00:00:15.862 --> 00:00:17.074
and downstream APIs.

00:00:17.074 --> 00:00:21.924
The variety of contexts is what makes injection prevention a design discipline

00:00:21.924 --> 00:00:23.944
rather than a single trick.

00:00:23.944 --> 00:00:27.178
The most important habit is data flow awareness.

00:00:27.178 --> 00:00:32.431
Know where untrusted data enters, how it is transformed, where it is stored,

00:00:32.431 --> 00:00:34.856
and which sensitive sinks it reaches.

00:00:34.856 --> 00:00:38.898
Client-side validation can improve usability, but server-side validation, safe construction,

00:00:38.898 --> 00:00:41.727
and context-aware output handling are still required.

00:00:41.727 --> 00:00:46.981
A value that is safe in one context may be unsafe in another.

00:00:46.981 --> 00:00:49.001
Strong prevention combines several controls.

00:00:49.001 --> 00:00:51.022
Use parameterized queries for SQL.

00:00:51.022 --> 00:00:54.659
Validate object shape and allowlisted fields for document queries.

00:00:54.659 --> 00:00:56.276
Avoid string-built shell commands.

00:00:56.276 --> 00:00:59.913
Keep user content separate from templates and executable logic.

00:00:59.913 --> 00:01:03.955
Use safe DOM APIs and context-aware encoding for browser rendering.

00:01:03.955 --> 00:01:08.804
Apply least privilege so one mistake does not become a system-wide failure.

00:01:08.804 --> 00:01:11.633
Testing and review keep these controls alive.

00:01:11.633 --> 00:01:16.079
Security-focused unit and integration tests, schema tests, negative tests, dependency review,

00:01:16.079 --> 00:01:20.120
static analysis, and code review all help identify risky paths.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:25.374
Documentation helps future developers understand why a particular sink needs a particular control.

00:01:25.374 --> 00:01:27.799
The goal is not memorizing payloads.

00:01:27.799 --> 00:01:33.053
The goal is designing JavaScript applications where untrusted data cannot change command meaning.

00:01:33.053 --> 00:01:37.499
When developers keep data as data, choose safe APIs, validate intent,

00:01:37.499 --> 00:01:41.540
and encode for the correct context, injection risk drops dramatically.
